extends RefCounted
static func teaching(topic: int) -> Dictionary:
	var equipment := [
		"FIND THE PARTS FIRST\n• A lead is one cable with a plug at each end. Take one cable at a time from a colored bag in the drawer UNDER your workbench. Click the drawer handle to open it. Click a bag, click the first socket, then click the second socket. Repeat for the next cable.\n• Power Supply: bottom-left device. Its variable outputs are the SECOND vertical column of sockets, marked 4, 5, 6 and N. Socket 4 is the RED socket at the TOP of that column. N is the pale socket at the BOTTOM of the SAME column. Do not use socket 1 in the first column.\n• Variable Resistance: second row, left. Use the LEFT group of three switches and its two RED sockets. Turn on only its MIDDLE switch (600 Ω).\n• AC Ammeter: top-left device. Use the LEFT meter and its RED sockets: ± is the first socket; 0.5 is the second.\n• AC Voltmeter: top-right device. Use the LEFT meter: black ± is first; yellow 250 is third.\n\nCONNECT FIVE CABLES — ONE AT A TIME\nStart with the Power Supply switch at O (off) and its large dial at 0.\n1. Power Supply red 4 → left Ammeter red ±.\n2. Left Ammeter red 0.5 → Variable Resistance UPPER red socket.\n3. Variable Resistance LOWER red socket → Power Supply N below 4/5/6.\n4. Left Voltmeter black ± → that SAME Power Supply N. Stack this plug on the cable already there.\n5. Left Voltmeter yellow 250 → Variable Resistance UPPER red socket. Stack it on the cable already there.\n\nREAD THE METERS\nTurn the supply switch to I (on). Raise the large dial slowly. Stop when the voltmeter shows the voltage asked for in the question. Read current from the left ammeter. Write both readings on your paper.\nFor the example on the next page, aim for 90 V. Current should be about 0.15 A. Use the question’s own values for your answers, not the example values.",
		"1. Use the variable AC output and a voltmeter connected across terminal 4 and N. The meter reads RMS, not peak voltage.\n2. Select the source frequency with [ and ]. Record RMS voltage and frequency.\n3. Draw the waveform and mark one period, positive peak, negative peak, and the zero axis. A negative instantaneous voltage is not a negative RMS reading.\n4. Use the calculator in DEG mode for phase angles. Convert milliseconds to seconds before applying f = 1/T.",
		"1. With power OFF, connect variable terminal 4 → ammeter → R-bank top. Connect R-bank bottom → L-bank top, then L-bank bottom → N.\n2. Choose one resistor and one inductor branch. For an RLC exercise insert the capacitor bank into the same series path.\n3. Set the meter range above your predicted current. Connect the voltmeter across the part you want to measure.\n4. Power ON from zero output and raise to the target voltage. The same current passes through each component; their voltage magnitudes need not add to the supply voltage.",
		"1. Power OFF. Use stacked plugs to make a common supply junction and a common return junction. Connect R and L banks between these same two nodes; add C only when the exercise calls for it.\n2. Place the source ammeter before the junction to measure total current. To measure an individual branch, power OFF and insert the meter only in that branch.\n3. Connect a voltmeter across the common nodes. All branches have this voltage.\n4. Compare the measured total current with the phasor sum, not with the sum of branch-current magnitudes.",
		"1. Sketch the nodes first. Build the specified series portion, then split the path into the parallel branches and rejoin them at the return. Keep track of which resistor belongs in which section.\n2. Insert the main ammeter before the split. Put the voltmeter across the parallel section when checking its voltage.\n3. Reduce the parallel impedances before adding series impedances. Do not add all resistor and reactance magnitudes together.\n4. Use available bank combinations for the physical circuit and calculate with their actual values; a randomized calculation case may not exactly match a switch combination.",
		"1. Power OFF. Build three equal R/L branches. For wye, join the three branch returns into the star point and connect the other ends to A, B and C.\n2. Measure line-to-line voltage with a suitable range, then line-to-neutral voltage. Insert each ammeter in a line.\n3. A wattmeter's current coil goes in series and its voltage coil across the designated nodes; follow the supplied two-wattmeter diagram for polarity.\n4. Sum the two signed wattmeter readings for total real power. A negative reading at low power factor is meaningful; do not discard its sign.",
		"1. Measure the balanced load's voltage, current, and total real power before correction. Keep the load unchanged.\n2. Calculate the reactive power to remove, then the capacitance per phase. Check whether the capacitors are connected in wye or delta: each sees a different voltage.\n3. Power OFF before adding equal capacitor banks in parallel with the three phases. Use achievable bank values and predict their actual correction.\n4. Power ON and compare line current and power factor. Real load power should remain approximately unchanged while reactive source current decreases.",
		"1. Identify the three motor winding pairs 1–4, 2–5 and 3–6. With power OFF, connect the appropriate wye or delta arrangement for the exercise.\n2. Start with the dynamometer load at minimum. Energize the correctly connected three-phase source.\n3. Increase mechanical load gradually. Hold the dynamometer's measuring control to read speed and modeled shaft torque.\n4. Calculate synchronous speed from poles and frequency. The rotor normally turns below this speed; loading increases slip. The game uses an approximate steady-state motor model, not a transient starting simulation.",
		"1. Locate primary 1–2 (120 V). The secondary chain 3–7–8–4 has 104 V, 76 V and 28 V sections; 5–9–6 has two 60 V sections.\n2. With power OFF, connect the selected primary to the source and a voltmeter across the chosen secondary taps. Do not short a winding.\n3. Start at zero variable voltage, energize, and compare the measured voltage ratio with the selected winding-rating ratio.\n4. Power OFF, add a resistor across the secondary and a series ammeter, then repeat. The ideal model reflects secondary load current into the primary; the winding does not change frequency.",
		"1. Identify DC input 1/2, output midpoints 3/4/5, and the six switching-control inputs on the inverter panel. Each leg has upper and lower switching devices with feedback diodes.\n2. Trace one diagonal conducting path on the diagram, then the opposite diagonal path. These reverse the voltage across the load.\n3. Never command both devices of one leg ON together: that would short the DC link. Dead time is the gap between turn-off and turn-on.\n4. This lesson is a calculation/diagram exercise: the game's AC solver does not generate inverter PWM waveforms. Use the stated ideal waveform for the questions.",
		"1. Identify the coil, turns N, flux direction and core area on the diagram. Mark the induced polarity that opposes an increase in flux.\n2. Convert area to square metres, flux to webers and time to seconds. Milliseconds and milliw eb ers must not be used directly in SI formulas.\n3. Use B = Φ/A for flux density, NI for magnetomotive force, and NΔΦ/Δt for induced emf magnitude.\n4. This is a conceptual calculation exercise. The visible transformer/motor cores illustrate where the magnetic field acts; the game does not simulate a spatial magnetic field."
	]
	var worked := [
		"At 90 V across 600 Ω: I = V/R = 90/600 = 0.150 A. Choose the 0.5 A range, not 2.5 A, for better resolution. P = VI = 90 × 0.150 = 13.5 W. If 300 Ω is switched in parallel, 1/Req = 1/600 + 1/300, so Req = 200 Ω and I = 0.450 A. An ammeter across the supply would act as a low-resistance path, not a voltage measurement.",
		"For 120 V RMS at 60 Hz: Vpk = 120√2 = 169.71 V. T = 1/60 = 0.016667 s = 16.667 ms. For 80 + j60 V: magnitude = √(80² + 60²) = 100 V, and φ = atan(60/80) = 36.87°. Enter atan(60/80) with DEG selected. Rectangular components add before converting back to magnitude and angle.",
		"Let V = 120 V, R = 300 Ω, XL = 400 Ω, XC = 0. Z = 300 + j400 Ω, |Z| = 500 Ω. I = 120/500 = 0.240 A. VR = IR = 72 V and VL = IXL = 96 V. √(72² + 96²) = 120 V. P = I²R = 17.28 W; PF = R/|Z| = 0.6 lagging. If a capacitor has XC = 100 Ω, replace net reactance by 400 − 100 before recalculating.",
		"At 120 V with R = 600 Ω and XL = 300 Ω: IR = 0.2 A and IL = −j0.4 A. I = 0.2 − j0.4 A, so |I| = 0.4472 A. P = V²/R = 24 W and Q = V²/XL = 48 var. PF = 24/√(24² + 48²) = 0.4472 lagging. Adding XC = 600 Ω supplies +j0.2 A, leaving total current 0.2 − j0.2 A, magnitude 0.2828 A.",
		"For 300 Ω in parallel with −j300 Ω: Yp = 1/300 + 1/(−j300) = 0.003333 + j0.003333 S. Zp = 1/Yp = 150 − j150 Ω. Add a series 150 Ω resistor and j150 Ω inductor: Ztotal = 300 + j0 Ω. At 90 V, I = 0.300 A. Vparallel = I Zp = 45 − j45 V; its magnitude is 63.64 V. Keep the complex signs through each step.",
		"For a balanced wye load at VL = 208 V, IL = 0.5 A, PF = 0.8: Vphase = 208/√3 = 120.09 V. S = √3 VL IL = 180.13 VA. P = S PF = 144.11 W. Q = √(S² − P²) = 108.08 var. With the convention Q = √3(W2 − W1), W1 = (P − Q/√3)/2 = 40.85 W and W2 = 103.25 W. Their sum is the real power.",
		"Suppose P = 300 W and initial PF = 0.6. Qold = P tan(acos(0.6)) = 400 var. For target PF = 0.9, Qtarget = 145.30 var, so Qc = 254.70 var. At 60 Hz and 120 V per phase in wye: Cphase = Qc/(3 × 2π × 60 × 120²) = 15.64 µF. In delta, use the line voltage across each capacitor. Do not confuse the total Qc with a per-phase value.",
		"For a four-pole motor at 60 Hz: ns = 120 × 60 / 4 = 1800 rpm. If nr = 1728 rpm, s = (1800 − 1728)/1800 = 0.04 = 4%. Rotor electrical frequency = sf = 2.4 Hz. At 200 W shaft output, ω = 2π × 1728 / 60 = 180.96 rad/s, so T = P/ω = 1.105 N·m. Use rotor speed, not synchronous speed, for shaft power.",
		"For a 120:60 V winding ratio, a = Np/Ns = 2. At Vp = 100 V, Vs = 50 V. A 200 Ω secondary load draws Is = 50/200 = 0.25 A. Ip = Is/a = 0.125 A, and Zin = a²R = 800 Ω. Check the ideal power balance: 100 × 0.125 = 50 × 0.25 = 12.5 W. Tap ratings determine ratios; they are not fixed output voltages regardless of the input.",
		"For an ideal ±100 V bipolar square wave at 2 kHz: T = 1/2000 = 500 µs. Each polarity lasts 250 µs at 50% duty. Vrms = √(average(v²)) = 100 V, because v² is always 10000. Across 200 Ω, P = 100²/200 = 50 W. A square wave's peak-to-RMS relation is not the sinusoidal √2 rule. Practical PWM and losses require a different model.",
		"A 500-turn coil changes flux by 0.001 Wb in 0.02 s: |e| = 500 × 0.001 / 0.02 = 25 V. If core area is 0.002 m², ΔB = 0.001/0.002 = 0.5 T. At 0.4 A, MMF = NI = 200 A·turns. Lenz's law sets polarity to oppose the flux change. Flux, flux density and MMF are different quantities with different units."
	]
	return {"equipment":equipment[clampi(topic,0,10)].replace("milliw eb ers","milliwebers"),"worked":worked[clampi(topic,0,10)]}

static func teaching_course(course_id: String,kind: String,week: int,duration_seconds: int=420) -> Dictionary:
	var result: Dictionary=load("res://scripts/campus/courses/course_provider.gd").lesson(course_id,week,kind,duration_seconds)
	if not result.has("error"):
		result.equipment_list=result.equipment
		result.equipment=result.equipment_text
	return result
